  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 112 AM CDT Sun Sep 20 2026\n\nRidging aloft will translate eastward across the region Tuesday. \nThis should help to bring a brief pause in rain chances. \nTemperatures will remain cooler than average as the low-level flow \nwill be originating from the high pressure across southeast Canada. \nHighs Tuesday will struggle into the upper 50s across north central \nNebraska with lower to mid 60s expected across the rest of the area. \nStill rather cool Wednesday despite winds shifting to the south-\nsoutheast. A shortwave will lift from the central Rockies to the \nNorthern Plains by Wednesday night. The airmass will be rather dry \nwith the origins from Canada, but some Gulf moisture may try and mix \nin across the western High Plains as the easterly flow across Texas \nturns northward. This may allow for scattered late afternoon \nconvection to develop late Wednesday afternoon and Wednesday night \nas weak cold front moves southeast into the area. Most ENS members \nand the operational ECMWF then build a ridge of high pressure into \nthe region Thursday into Friday. High temperatures moderate into the \n70s as this occurs.",
